{% doc %}
  The ruled term-and-value treatment shared by the product specification ledger
  and the material comparison table.

  This snippet renders nothing. Shopify subsets `{% stylesheet %}` CSS to the
  files in a page's render tree, so styles shared by sections that never render
  together have to be pulled in by something each of them renders. Keeping them
  in `critical.css` instead would ship them to every page in the theme.

  The classes are deliberately element-agnostic: the ledger puts them on
  `<dt>`/`<dd>`, the comparison table on `<th scope="row">`/`<td>`. Only the
  rule, the rhythm and the two type treatments live here -- the grid or table
  that arranges them belongs to whichever file is doing the arranging.

  @example
  {% render 'styles-ledger' %}
{% enddoc %}

{% stylesheet %}
.ledger__term,
.ledger__value {
padding-block: var(--space-4);
border-block-end: 1px solid var(--hairline-row);
}

.ledger__term {
padding-inline-end: var(--space-8);
font-size: var(--text-label);
font-weight: var(--font-body--weight-medium);
line-height: 1.6;
letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
text-transform: uppercase;
text-align: start;
color: var(--color-secondary);
}

.ledger__value {
margin: 0;
font-size: var(--text-detail);
line-height: 1.6;
color: var(--color-heading);
}
{% endstylesheet %}
